{"name":"Citrus Hill","hex":"#f9a78d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"citrus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several shrubs or trees of the genus Citrus in the family Rutaceae.","The fruit of such plants, generally spherical, oblate, or prolate, consisting of an outer glandular skin (called zest), an inner white skin (called pith or albedo), and generally between 8 and 16 sectors filled with pulp consisting of cells with one end attached to the inner skin. Citrus fruits include orange, grapefruit, lemon, lime, and citron."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or similar to citrus plants or fruit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/citrus"},{"term":"hill","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elevated landmass smaller than a mountain.","A sloping part of a landscape, especially one with a road, trail, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form into a heap or mound.","To heap or draw earth around plants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hill"}],"entries":[{"title":"Citrus Hill","blocks":[{"text":"Citrus Hill was a brand of orange juice introduced by Procter & Gamble in the United States market in 1982, later used for other fruit juices and beverages."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citrus_Hill"},{"title":"Citrus","blocks":[{"text":"Citrus is a genus of flowering trees and shrubs in the family Rutaceae. Plants in the genus produce citrus fruits, such as citrons, mandarins, and pomelos. Many important citrus crops have been developed through extensive hybridization, including oranges, lemons, grapefruits, and limes, all of which have many cultivars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/OrangeBloss_wb.jpg/330px-OrangeBloss_wb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citrus"},{"title":"Hill","blocks":[{"text":"A hill is a landform that extends above the surrounding terrain. It often has a distinct summit, and is usually applied to peaks which are above elevation compared to the relative landmass, though not as prominent as mountains. Hills fall under the category of slope landforms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Uliveti_monte_cinto.JPG/330px-Uliveti_monte_cinto.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hill"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Ichangfruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Ichangfruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIchangfruit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Quinotos.jpg/330px-Quinotos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Quinotos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Quinotos.jpg/330px-Quinotos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuinotos.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Buddhas_hand_1.jpg/250px-Buddhas_hand_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Buddhas_hand_1.jpg/500px-Buddhas_hand_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Buddhas_hand_1.jpg/250px-Buddhas_hand_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wi